==================================================================
DAY 6 - PART A: THEORY
The Request Flow: What Happens When You Run "kubectl run"
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. THE 8 STEPS
--------------
When you type:   k run web --image=nginx

 1. kubectl reads ~/.kube/config and sends an authenticated HTTPS
    POST request to the API server.

 2. The API server runs three checks:
      Authentication  - who are you? (certificate, token)
      Authorization   - are you allowed? (RBAC)
      Admission       - validate or modify the request (defaults,
                        quotas, policies)

 3. The API server stores the Pod object in etcd.
    The pod now EXISTS, but has no node assigned (status: Pending).

 4. The scheduler, which is watching the API server, sees a pod with
    no node. It filters nodes that fit, scores them, and writes the
    chosen node name into the pod (spec.nodeName). This is "binding".

 5. The kubelet on that node, also watching, sees a pod assigned to it.

 6. The kubelet asks containerd (via CRI) to pull the image and start
    the container. The CNI plugin gives the pod an IP address.

 7. The kubelet reports status (Running, pod IP) back to the API
    server, which saves it in etcd.

 8. "kubectl get pods" reads that status from the API server.


2. THE KEY INSIGHT: NOBODY CALLS ANYBODY
----------------------------------------
The scheduler does not call the kubelet. The kubelet does not call the
scheduler. Every component WATCHES the API server and reacts to
changes. This "hub and spoke" design makes the system loosely coupled
and resilient: if the scheduler restarts, it just catches up.

   kubectl --POST--> API server <--watch-- scheduler
                        |   ^
                      etcd  +----watch---- kubelet (each node)


3. spec vs status
-----------------
Every Kubernetes object has two halves:
  spec    = what you WANT   (written by you)
  status  = what EXISTS     (written by Kubernetes)
This is the desired/actual model from Day 5, inside every object.


4. POD PHASES AND COMMON STATUSES
---------------------------------
Phases:  Pending -> Running -> Succeeded or Failed  (also Unknown)

Statuses you will see in "k get pods":
  ContainerCreating   image is being pulled / container starting
  Running             at least one container is running
  Completed           container exited with code 0
  ErrImagePull        first failure pulling the image
  ImagePullBackOff    retrying the pull with growing delays
  CrashLoopBackOff    container keeps starting and crashing
  Pending             not scheduled yet (no node fits) or waiting


5. EVENTS
---------
Kubernetes records what happens to objects as Events:
  Scheduled -> Pulling -> Pulled -> Created -> Started
Events are the FIRST place to look when something is wrong.
They expire after about 1 hour.


6. A BARE POD HAS NO BABYSITTER
-------------------------------
A pod created with "k run" is not managed by any controller.
If you delete it, or its node dies, nothing recreates it.
That is why real apps use Deployments (Week 2).


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- API server stores; scheduler picks a node; kubelet runs it.
- All components watch the API server instead of calling each other.
- Debug habit: describe first (events), then logs.
